    <h2 class="h1 mb-4">History of Sand Point Way NE</h2>
    <p>
      Sand Point Way NE is one of northeast Seattle's oldest through-routes, and its history explains a lot about why the street looks the way it does today. It was built to serve a military base, spent more than fifty years as a state highway, and is only now, decades after that highway designation was partially removed, being redesigned with everyday walkers, cyclists, and bus riders in mind.
    </p>
    <h2 class="h2 mt-4 mb-3">Built for the Navy, not the neighborhood</h2>
    <p>
        The road's modern history begins with the U.S. Navy. In 1926, King County transferred roughly 413 acres at Sand Point to the federal government, and the Navy established Naval Air Station Sand Point the following year. The new base needed reliable road access, so the City of Seattle officially named Sand Point Way and worked with King County to grade and pave it, giving the growing base a real connection to the rest of the city.
    </p>
    <p>
        In its early decades, the road ran from 45th Street and Union Bay Place NE north to the Naval Air Station's gate at NE 74th Street- roughly where the entrance to present-day Magnuson Park sits. A modest strip of small businesses grew up alongside it in the 1930s, including gas stations, corner grocery stores, and taverns such as Dooley's Tavern, though most of these small operations lasted only a few years. North of the base gate, Sand Point Way narrowed to a two-lane country road that hugged the Lake Washington shoreline on its way toward what eventually became Lake City.
    <h2 class="h2 mt-4 mb-3">Fifty-four years as a state highway</h2>
    <p>
        Sand Point Way's life as a state route began in 1937, when Washington designated it (together with Montlake Boulevard and University Way) as part of Secondary State Highway 1J, a 13.4-mile route connecting Montlake to points north. The 1964 statewide highway renumbering turned SSH 1J into State Route 513.
    </p>
    <p>
        SR 513 didn't stay the same size for long. In 1971, the state shortened the route to 9.22 miles, anchoring its south end at the SR 520 interchange in Montlake and its north end at Interstate 5 — meaning the highway designation still extended north past Magnuson Park, through the Lake City area, before meeting I-5. That changed in 1991, when the state truncated SR 513 again, this time down to its current 3.35 miles. The section running north from Magnuson Park to I-5, the same stretch this project focuses on, was relinquished by the state and handed to the City of Seattle as an ordinary city street.
    </p>
    <p>
        That 1991 handoff is the origin of this project's name. The portion of Sand Point Way NE north of Magnuson Park is literally the piece of road that moved "beyond SR 513" — it left state ownership more than three decades ago, but its design (wide travel lanes, few crossings, minimal sidewalks) has changed only modestly since.
    </p> 
    <h2 class="h2 mt-4 mb-3">The base closes, the neighborhood changes</h2>
    <p>
        As the Naval Air Station wound down in the postwar decades, the small commercial strip along Sand Point Way never grew into a real neighborhood business district. By the 1960s, shoppers had shifted to the larger, consolidated stores at the Lake City commercial core near NE 125th Street and Lake City Way NE, and the isolated corner stores and gas stations north of the old base gate gradually disappeared. The former base itself became Warren G. Magnuson Park, named for the longtime Washington U.S. Senator, and today anchors the corridor's south end with trails, playfields, and direct access to the Burke-Gilman Trail.
    </p> 
    <h2 class="h2 mt-4 mb-3">Levy-funded improvements: from Bridging the Gap to Move Seattle</h2>
    <p>
        Seattle voters have funded two major transportation levies since the mid-2000s, and both left a mark on this corridor's maintenance and safety record even before the current push for redesign.
    </p> 
    <h3 class="h3 mt-4 mb-3">Bridging the Gap (2006&ndash;2015)</h3>
    <p>
        Approved by voters in 2006, the nine-year, $365 million Bridging the Gap levy directed most of its funding (at least 67%) to basic maintenance (paving, sidewalk repair, bridge work, and tree care) with a minimum 18% set aside for pedestrian, bicycle, and school-safety programs, and up to 15% for transit and larger capital projects. Along Sand Point Way NE, this era's investment was mostly maintenance-level: paving and spot repairs rather than the crossing and sidewalk overhaul the corridor actually needed.
    </p> 
    <h3 class="h3 mt-4 mb-3">Levy to Move Seattle (2016&ndash;2024)</h3>
    <p>
        Voters approved the much larger, nine-year, $930 million Levy to Move Seattle in November 2015. Citywide, it funded more than 250 blocks of new sidewalks, roughly 68 miles of new bike facilities, three new bridges, and three RapidRide transit corridors. It's also the funding source behind the Sand Point Way NE Corridor Project — the direct predecessor to today's advocacy effort.
    </p>
    <p>
        That corridor project took shape over several years: a neighborhood grant application in 2016 led to an SDOT transportation study in 2017, followed by public outreach, design, and construction between 2018 and 2021. The finished work included:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>New curb bulbs and sidewalk sections at NE 123rd St</li>
        <li>Removed slip lanes and new pedestrian-activated flashing beacons at NE 95th St</li>
        <li>New pedestrian-activated flashing beacons near NE NOAA Dr</li>
        <li>Signal-timing upgrades to improve pedestrian crossings at Princeton Ave NE, NE 65th St, NE 70th St, and NE 74th St</li>
        <li>Bike-connection improvements near NE 77th St</li>
    </ul>
    <h2 class="h2 mt-4 mb-3">Where things stand today</h2>
    <p>
        The Levy to Move Seattle-funded work improved specific pinch points, but it stopped well short of the continuous sidewalks, protected crossings, and slower design speeds this corridor still needs, especially on the stretch north of Magnuson Park that has been a city street, not a state highway, since 1991. Seattle's transportation levies have since moved on (Move Seattle wrapped up in 2024, replaced by a new, larger transportation levy), which means the next round of funding decisions is exactly the moment for this corridor's remaining gaps to be heard.
    </p>
    <p>
        That history is the whole premise behind Beyond SR 513: this road has already moved beyond its highway-era ownership. It's time for its design to catch up.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
